window.__labLoaded({"id":"1.5","title":"Z-Scores","goal":"Use z-scores to compare Maya's Statistics and Biology marks, and standardise the lesson's five quiz scores.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Compare Maya's two exams","text":"A z-score is (score - class mean) divided by class SD: how many standard deviations above or below average. Raw marks of 85 and 78 cannot be compared, but z-scores can.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nz_stat = (85 - 70) / 10\nz_bio = (78 - 65) / 6\n\nprint(f'Statistics: z = {z_stat:.2f}')\nprint(f'Biology:    z = {z_bio:.2f}')\nprint('Maya did better, relative to her class, in', 'Biology' if z_bio > z_stat else 'Statistics')","out":"Statistics: z = 1.50\nBiology:    z = 2.17\nMaya did better, relative to her class, in Biology","figs":[]},{"title":"Standardise a whole data set","text":"stats.zscore does every value at once. It divides by the population SD unless you set ddof=1, which gives the lesson's sample-SD values (-0.86, 0.26 ...).","code":"score = np.array([72, 85, 90, 68, 95])\n\nz = stats.zscore(score, ddof=1)\nprint('z (ddof=1):', z.round(2))\nprint('z (default ddof=0):', stats.zscore(score).round(2))","out":"z (ddof=1): [-0.86  0.26  0.69 -1.21  1.12]\nz (default ddof=0): [-0.96  0.29  0.77 -1.35  1.25]","figs":[]},{"title":"Mean 0 and SD 1","text":"Standardised data always have mean 0 and standard deviation 1. The shape and the ranking do not change, only the scale.","code":"print(f'mean of z = {abs(z.mean()):.2f}')\nprint(f'sd of z   = {z.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint('same order as the scores?', (np.argsort(z) == np.argsort(score)).all())","out":"mean of z = 0.00\nsd of z   = 1.00\nsame order as the scores? True","figs":[]},{"title":"Back to raw scores and percentiles","text":"Reverse the formula with x = mean + z x SD. For bell-shaped data, norm.cdf turns a z-score into the share of values below it. Only trust that if the histogram is roughly bell-shaped.","code":"print('IQ with z = 2:', 100 + 2 * 15)\nprint(f'share below z = 1.5: {stats.norm.cdf(1.5):.3f}')\nprint(f'share below z = 2.17: {stats.norm.cdf(z_bio):.3f}')","out":"IQ with z = 2: 130\nshare below z = 1.5: 0.933\nshare below z = 2.17: 0.985","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"score <- c(%s)\nscale(score)[, 1]\n(85 - 70) / 10\n(78 - 65) / 6\npnorm(1.5)","expect":"z for the five scores: -0.86, 0.26, 0.69, -1.21, 1.12; Maya z = 1.5 (Statistics) and 2.17 (Biology); pnorm(1.5) = 0.9332","note":"scale() divides by the sample SD, the same as ddof=1. It returns a matrix, so [, 1] pulls out the plain vector."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives, then tick 'Save standardized values as variables'","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / score.\nBEGIN DATA\n72  85  90  68  95\nEND DATA.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=score\n  /SAVE.\nLIST VARIABLES=score Zscore.","note":"/SAVE adds a new variable named Zscore using the sample SD, so the values are -0.86, 0.26, 0.69, -1.21 and 1.12. LIST prints the scores next to them."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput score\n72\n85\n90\n68\n95\nend\negen z = std(score)\nlist score z\ndisplay (85 - 70) / 10\ndisplay (78 - 65) / 6","note":"egen std() uses the sample SD (n - 1), so z matches the lesson. The two display lines are Maya's z-scores, 1.5 and 2.1667."},"try":["Change the Statistics mark from 85 to 88 and predict whether Maya's Statistics z-score passes her Biology z-score.","Add the value 150 to the five scores. Predict how that changes the z-score of 95, then run it."]});
